00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:01.270 align:start position:0%
 
[Applause]  sheikh sharif ahmed was president of  somalia once before and he wants to be  president again  his government was credited with  developing somalia's constitution and  building the national army after the  central government collapsed in 1991  it was criticized for corruption  i want to build a government that gives  equal rights to all the somali people  and provides security and justice to the  somali people  he's more known for trying to include  all political factions than for specific  policies  sheikh sharif is widely seen as one of  the strongest contenders he's one of 39  candidates who have registered their  posters line this street in mogadishu  one of them will go on to lead a  government that doesn't control much of  somalia beyond here the capital sways of  the countryside are controlled by the  armed group al-shabab somalis don't even  get to vote in this election it's  members of parliament who will elect the  president and the members of parliament  were selected by clan representatives  everyone familiar with the process say  it's been marred by vote buying and  corruption  another former president hassan sheikh  muhammad was in the running and also  widely seen as a possible winner  when he was in office un investigators  said private individuals embezzled vast  sums of money from the central bank  once a peace activist he's known as a  negotiator and says he'll mend the rifts  between somalia's federal state  incumbent president mohammed abdullahi  farmer is running again  he's lost support in parliament his  opponents say he's divisive made  somalia's armed conflicts worse and  brought economic decline  he's promising change  hassan ali khairi was prime minister  under farmer until they fell out he's  also a prominent contender  the final outcome of the vote depends on  who ends up supporting who  it's very difficult to predict  you know potential alliances at this  stage i think a lot of back room deals  and under the table deals are happening  as we speak and they'll continue to  happen until the 11th hour  just before dawn on sunday morning  they'll continue to make deals  members of parliament will vote on  sunday in a series of up to three rounds  in the safety of mogadishu's green zone  far removed from most somalis  the region's worst drought in decades is  just one of the many challenges faced by  millions of people and by somalia's next  leader  malcolm webb al jazeera mogadishu  somalia

